{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion lag: The model consistently fails to anticipate the sharp price drop from ~80 to ~67 occurring 10-15 days after the forecast date. When history shows a recent peak followed by a decline, immediately reduce the forecast trajectory by 10-15% rather than assuming a slow drift.",
    "Adjust for sentiment disconnect: Positive sentiment spikes (values > 0.20) in the history do not correlate with immediate price increases in the true outcome; the model overweights these signals. Treat sentiment > 0.20 as a noise indicator rather than a bullish trigger.",
    "Apply overshoot correction for recovery phases: The model underestimates the magnitude of the rebound (from ~67 back to ~80) in the final 5-7 days of the horizon. When the true outcome shows a bottoming pattern, increase the forecast slope significantly to capture the V-shaped recovery.",
    "Reduce forecast persistence: The model exhibits excessive inertia, projecting the last observed price with minimal variance. Replace the flat/slow-drift projection with a higher volatility band that accounts for the observed 15-point swing range.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: Systematically overestimate prices in the first 5 days of the forecast window when the immediate history is volatile, and systematically underestimate prices in the 20-30 day window during recovery periods."
  ]
}
